/**
 * @page topic_client Client Application
 * @ingroup module_client
 * @brief Client architecture from input selection to rendered conversation.
 *
 * The client is a composition of shared runtimes rather than a collection of
 * protocol-specific send threads. Options select media and endpoint behavior;
 * the session owns producers and the network connection; the TUI presents
 * snapshots.
 *
 * @section client_layers Layers
 *
 * - `src/client/` orchestrates client mode and media producers.
 * - `lib/network/` owns connections, transports, ACIP packets, negotiation, and
 *   backpressure.
 * - `lib/media/`, audio, and video modules capture/decode/encode media.
 * - `lib/tui/` and `lib/ui/` present live state and handle interaction.
 *
 * @section client_flow Flow
 *
 * 1. Parse options and initialize platform/media capabilities.
 * 2. Establish and authenticate one `network_connection_t`.
 * 3. Start capture/audio producers after the connection is ready.
 * 4. Send through type-specific ACIP helpers and the shared scheduler.
 * 5. Decode incoming ACIP media into playback/render snapshots.
 * 6. Stop producers, close the connection, and restore the terminal in order.
 *
 * @section client_ownership Ownership rules
 *
 * The connection owns its transport and handshake state. Producers own capture
 * buffers until the send API returns; queued transports retain their own copies.
 * Borrowed receive payloads are valid only during the packet callback. Client
 * mode must not add a socket loop, global send mutex, or unbounded media queue.
 *
 * @see topic_client_main
 * @see topic_client_connection
 * @see topic_client_capture
 * @see topic_client_audio
 */
